 | D-D55 | Taken 2026-10-29 (the maintainer's requests and answers at 18:18, 19:55 and 23:21 UTC, logged in short in the session's log): (1) at 18:18 UTC, "There a lot of good lesson learned, like with the foot problems, just want to make sure we capture everything we have tried": one record of everything tried on the T. rex tail saturation habit since 2026-07-09 (made by the session: drafted locally from three source sweeps, read by a completeness critic and a number-by-number fact check and fixed, then brought up to date with (2) and D-D54 (4) and fact-checked again); (2) at 19:55 UTC, asked whether the habit reflects a body positioning that encourages it, "Yes, queue them up": three no-training posture tests (whether a posture shift came before the tail rose, across every local run record; whether the policies' tail command responds to posture inputs; whether the plant pushes the tail up, or makes tail-up cheaper, in the posture the railed lineage holds), with an adversarial verifier; (3) at 23:21 UTC, "Own note + evidence PR": that record as its own dated investigation note, with its ledger, the day's evidence and the run log's lines from 721, in a records pull request: this change. Report only, the posture tests' outcome (verified with corrections): the plant gives no mechanical push (the tail map and the cost of tail-up are the same in every posture tested); no nose-down or forward centre-of-mass shift came before the tail rose, in any run with a tail signal before its rise (the verifier did not re-check the r9 runs); from mid-rise on, the railed lineage's networks read their nose-down posture as a tail offset, which the loop amplifies, a pathway absent before the rise. Nothing trains, and no gate, reward, plant or criterion changes. The record is `docs/investigations/TREX_TAIL_SATURATION_HABIT_2026_10.md`, its ledger in `docs/investigations/stance_2026_10/tail_record/`. |
+| D-D56 | Taken 2026-10-30 (the maintainer's answers at 00:24 and 00:45 UTC, logged in short in the session's log): (1) at 00:24 UTC, "Early-bias probe (Recommended)", with no training: at the first r10 round's local checkpoints one PPO update is rebuilt (rollout, GAE, the full PPO loss with its mirror term) with no optimizer step, and each loss term's first-order push on the deterministic `tail_3` command is split by reward term and state region and checked against the actual change between checkpoints, then read by an adversarial verifier; (2) at 00:45 UTC, going to bed, "Probes + one small screen (Recommended)": after the probe is verified, without asking again, (a) the no-training follow-ups (the probe's lead, and an early-warning curve of the tail loop's gain and offset over the saved checkpoints of r10 s42, s60 and s80 and the 24 entropy-screen continuations) and (b) one scratch CPU training screen: at most 9 continuations of at most 1.5M steps from `stance6_r10LTSB_s42`'s 1,048,576-step checkpoint, control against one intervention on the driver the probe names, its criteria written, reviewed and sha-pinned before launch, an outcome-blind runner, report only, nothing merged or pushed but a durable text copy; and the analysis put together. Report only, the outcomes (verified, with the verifiers' corrections): only the policy-gradient term moves the tail command's mean; at 1,310,720 steps, about 0.1M after s42's tail began to rise, s42 already has an upward push on `tail_3` carried by its own exploration noise, and s80 none; the critic's bootstrapped value is that push's source from 2,097,152 on, and unresolved at the onset. The tail loop's gain turns positive one checkpoint before the first tail over-bar episode in s42 and s60, but late s80 reaches the same loop state with none, so no loop-only rule separates the runs with any margin; the one warning signal testable on the r9 runs, the policy probe's `tail_3` offset, fired in all five by 2.06M, the cleanest negative included, so the r9 held-out test fails; and late s80 is as close to the rail as s42 one checkpoint before its first over-bar episode, approaching it 4-12x more slowly. Under (2) (b) the session chose, at 04:22 UTC, `critic_blind_tail3`: the normalised `tail_3` angle (observation 6) set to 0 at the value network's input only, the actor still seeing it. The first critic-blind screen: 4 control and 4 blinded continuations from 1,048,576 to 2,490,368 steps (175 updates), seeds 2101 to 2131, its criteria reviewed (9 findings, none blocking) and fixed, approved at 06:00:05 UTC (sha256 `7bf25afd…`, criteria `9c490cc6…`) and launched at 06:00:19 UTC. All 8 COUNTED, and the scorer read, final, at 09:03 UTC: line 1, "NOT INFORMATIVE: THE CONTROL ARM DID NOT REPRODUCE THE EARLY RISE OF THE ON-POLICY TAIL_3 COMMAND (its median primary rose by less than 0.10 from the value at S, or no control primary was read); no reading about the critic is made": the control median rose +0.09184 against the pre-registered 0.10 (report only: p 13/70). Two independent checks, of the statistic and of the process, agreed with no blocking finding. From the same networks the early rise came in some seeds and not others under fresh rollout seeds (report only). The screen's text outputs went to the orphan branch `claude/handoff-bias-screen` at 09:17 UTC under (2), never to be merged. Screen runs certify nothing, and nothing trains on `main`. The evidence is `docs/investigations/stance_2026_10/overnight_probes/`, `bias_screen/` and `critic_blind_results/`; the record is `docs/investigations/TREX_TAIL_SATURATION_HABIT_2026_10.md` (§9). |
